{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"mcp_tool_call","server":"octo","tool":"query","arguments":{"folder":"/home/auras/Projects/Lean_Refactor/competition/projects/v4.33","query":"Prove bisimilarity using a symmetric relation and simulation in only one direction; bisimilarity itself is a bisimulation","topK":8},"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"scope=both folder=/home/auras/Projects/Lean_Refactor/competition/projects/v4.33\n\n1. **Cslib.LTS.HomBisimilarity.symm_simulation** (theorem, cslib, distance=0.667)\n   Homogeneous bisimilarity as symmetric simulation\n   For a labelled transition system $\\mathrm{lts}$ on a state type $S$ and a label type $L$, the homogeneous bisimilarity relation $\\mathrm{HomBisimilarity}$ between states $s_1$ and $s_2$ holds if and only if there exists a symmetric homogeneous simulation relation $r$ on $S$ such that $r$ relates $s_1$ and $s_2$. In other words, two states are bisimilar exactly when they are related by some symmetric simulation.\n   `Cslib.LTS.HomBisimilarity.symm_simulation.{u_4, u_5} {State✝ : Type u_4} {Label✝ : Type u_5}   {lts : Cslib.LTS State✝ Label✝} : lts.HomBisimilarity = fun s₁ s₂ => ∃ r, r s₁ s₂ ∧ Std.Symm r ∧ lts.IsHomSimulation r`\n   at: Cslib.Foundations.Semantics.LTS.Bisimulation:431\n   file: /home/auras/Projects/Lean_Refactor/competition/projects/v4.33/.lake/packages/cslib/Cslib/Foundations/Semantics/LTS/Bisimulation.lean\n\n2. **Cslib.LTS.IsBisimulation.isSimulation_iff** (theorem, cslib, distance=0.677)\n   Bisimulation iff both it and its inverse are simulations\n   For two labelled transition systems $\\mathrm{lts}_1$ and $\\mathrm{lts}_2$ on state types $\\mathrm{State}_1$ and $\\mathrm{State}_2$ with a common label type $\\mathrm{Label}$, and a relation $r : \\mathrm{State}_1 \\to \\mathrm{State}_2 \\to \\mathrm{Prop}$, the relation $r$ is a bisimulation between $\\mathrm{lts}_1$ and $\\mathrm{lts}_2$ if and only if $r$ is a simulation from $\\mathrm{lts}_1$ to $\\mathrm{lts}_2$ and the inverse relation $r^{-1}$ (defined by $r^{-1}(s_2, s_1) := r(s_1, s_2)$) is a simulation from $\\mathrm{lts}_2$ to $\\mathrm{lts}_1$.\n   `Cslib.LTS.IsBisimulation.isSimulation_iff.{u_1, u_2, u_3} {State₁ : Type u_1} {State₂ : Type u_2} {Label : Type u_3}   {lts₁ : Cslib.LTS State₁ Label} {lts₂ : Cslib.LTS State₂ Label} {r : State₁ → State₂ → Prop} :   lts₁.IsBisimulation lts₂ r ↔ lts₁.IsSimulation lts₂ r ∧ lts₂.IsSimulation lts₁ (flip r)`\n   at: Cslib.Foundations.Semantics.LTS.Bisimulation:104\n   file: /home/auras/Projects/Lean_Refactor/competition/projects/v4.33/.lake/packages/cslib/Cslib/Foundations/Semantics/LTS/Bisimulation.lean\n\n3. **Cslib.LTS.Bisimilarity.symm** (theorem, cslib, distance=0.706)\n   Symmetry of Bisimilarity\n   Bisimilarity is symmetric: for any labelled transition systems $\\mathrm{lts}_1$ and $\\mathrm{lts}_2$ on the same state type $S$ and label type $L$, if states $s_1$ and $s_2$ are bisimilar in $\\mathrm{lts}_1$ and $\\mathrm{lts}_2$ respectively, then $s_2$ and $s_1$ are bisimilar in $\\mathrm{lts}_2$ and $\\mathrm{lts}_1$ respectively.\n   `Cslib.LTS.Bisimilarity.symm.{u_3, u_4} {Label : Type u_3} {State : Type u_4} {lts₁ lts₂ : Cslib.LTS State Label}   {s₁ s₂ : State} (h : lts₁.Bisimilarity lts₂ s₁ s₂) : lts₂.Bisimilarity lts₁ s₂ s₁`\n   at: Cslib.Foundations.Semantics.LTS.Bisimulation:152\n   file: /home/auras/Projects/Lean_Refactor/competition/projects/v4.33/.lake/packages/cslib/Cslib/Foundations/Semantics/LTS/Bisimulation.lean\n\n4. **Cslib.LTS.IsBisimulation** (definition, cslib, distance=0.727)\n   Bisimulation relation for labelled transition systems\n   Given two labelled transition systems $\\mathrm{lts}_1$ and $\\mathrm{lts}_2$ on state types $\\mathrm{State}_1$ and $\\mathrm{State}_2$ with a common label type $\\mathrm{Label}$, a relation $r : \\mathrm{State}_1 \\to \\mathrm{State}_2 \\to \\mathrm{Prop}$ is a bisimulation if, whenever $r$ relates two states $s_1$ and $s_2$, then for every label $\\mu$, the following two conditions hold:  1. For every transition $s_1 \\xrightarrow{\\mu} s_1'$ in $\\mathrm{lts}_1$, there exists a transition $s_2 \\xrightarrow{\\mu} s_2'$ in $\\mathrm{lts}_2$ such that $r$ relates $s_1'$ and $s_2'$. 2. For every transition $s_2 \\xrightarrow{\\mu} s_2'$ in $\\mathrm{lts}_2$, there exists a transition $s_1 \\xrightarrow{\\mu} s_1'$ in $\\mathrm{lts}_1$ such that $r$ relates $s_1'$ and $s_2'$.  In other words, the transitions from related states mimic each other, and the reached derivatives are again related.\n   `Cslib.LTS.IsBisimulation.{u_1, u_2, u_3} {State₁ : Type u_1} {State₂ : Type u_2} {Label : Type u_3}   (lts₁ : Cslib.LTS State₁ Label) (lts₂ : Cslib.LTS State₂ Label) (r : State₁ → State₂ → Prop) : Prop`\n   at: Cslib.Foundations.Semantics.LTS.Bisimulation:75\n   file: /home/auras/Projects/Lean_Refactor/competition/projects/v4.33/.lake/packages/cslib/Cslib/Foundations/Semantics/LTS/Bisimulation.lean\n\n5. **Cslib.LTS.IsBisimulation.simulationEquiv** (theorem, cslib, distance=0.729)\n   Bisimulation implies simulation equivalence\n   If a relation $r$ between the states of two labelled transition systems is a bisimulation, then any two states related by $r$ are simulation equivalent.\n   `Cslib.LTS.IsBisimulation.simulationEquiv.{u_1, u_2, u_3} {State₁ : Type u_1} {State₂ : Type u_2} {Label : Type u_3}   {lts₁ : Cslib.LTS State₁ Label} {lts₂ : Cslib.LTS State₂ Label} {r : State₁ → State₂ → Prop} {s₁ : State₁}   {s₂ : State₂} (h : lts₁.IsBisimulation lts₂ r) (hrel : r s₁ s₂) : lts₁.SimulationEquiv lts₂ s₁ s₂`\n   at: Cslib.Foundations.Semantics.LTS.Bisimulation:187\n   file: /home/auras/Projects/Lean_Refactor/competition/projects/v4.33/.lake/packages/cslib/Cslib/Foundations/Semantics/LTS/Bisimulation.lean\n\n6. **Cslib.LTS.IsBisimulationUpTo** (definition, cslib, distance=0.730)\n   Bisimulation up to homogeneous bisimilarity\n   A relation $r$ between the states of two labelled transition systems $\\mathrm{lts}_1$ and $\\mathrm{lts}_2$ (with the same label type) is a bisimulation up to homogeneous bisimilarity if, whenever $r$ relates two states $s_1$ and $s_2$, then for every label $\\mu$, every transition from $s_1$ labelled by $\\mu$ to some $s_1'$ is matched by a transition from $s_2$ labelled by $\\mu$ to some $s_2'$ such that $s_1'$ and $s_2'$ are related by the relation 'up to homogeneous bisimilarity' (i.e., the composition of homogeneous bisimilarity on $\\mathrm{lts}_1$, the relation $r$, and homogeneous bisimilarity on $\\mathrm{lts}_2$), and symmetrically, every transition from $s_2$ labelled by $\\mu$ is matched by a transition from $s_1$ labelled by $\\mu$ to a state related up to homogeneous bisimilarity.\n   `Cslib.LTS.IsBisimulationUpTo.{u_1, u_2, u_3} {State₁ : Type u_1} {State₂ : Type u_2} {Label : Type u_3}   (lts₁ : Cslib.LTS State₁ Label) (lts₂ : Cslib.LTS State₂ Label) (r : State₁ → State₂ → Prop) : Prop`\n   at: Cslib.Foundations.Semantics.LTS.Bisimulation:269\n   file: /home/auras/Projects/Lean_Refactor/competition/projects/v4.33/.lake/packages/cslib/Cslib/Foundations/Semantics/LTS/Bisimulation.lean\n\n7. **Cslib.LTS.IsHomBisimulation** (definition, cslib, distance=0.733)\n   Homogeneous bisimulation relation for a labelled transition system\n   Given a labelled transition system $\\mathrm{lts}$ on a state type $\\mathrm{State}$ and a label type $\\mathrm{Label}$, a relation $r : \\mathrm{State} \\to \\mathrm{State} \\to \\mathrm{Prop}$ is a homogeneous bisimulation if it is a bisimulation between $\\mathrm{lts}$ and itself. That is, whenever $r$ relates two states $s_1$ and $s_2$, then for every label $\\mu$, the following two conditions hold:  1. For every transition $s_1 \\xrightarrow{\\mu} s_1'$ in $\\mathrm{lts}$, there exists a transition $s_2 \\xrightarrow{\\mu} s_2'$ in $\\mathrm{lts}$ such that $r$ relates $s_1'$ and $s_2'$. 2. For every transition $s_2 \\xrightarrow{\\mu} s_2'$ in $\\mathrm{lts}$, there exists a transition $s_1 \\xrightarrow{\\mu} s_1'$ in $\\mathrm{lts}$ such that $r$ relates $s_1'$ and $s_2'$.\n   `Cslib.LTS.IsHomBisimulation.{u_3, u_4} {Label : Type u_3} {State : Type u_4} (lts : Cslib.LTS State Label)   (r : State → State → Prop) : Prop`\n   at: Cslib.Foundations.Semantics.LTS.Bisimulation:110\n   file: /home/auras/Projects/Lean_Refactor/competition/projects/v4.33/.lake/packages/cslib/Cslib/Foundations/Semantics/LTS/Bisimulation.lean\n\n8. **Cslib.LTS.IsBisimulation.isSimulation** (theorem, cslib, distance=0.742)\n   Bisimulation implies simulation\n   For any labelled transition systems $\\mathrm{lts}_1$ and $\\mathrm{lts}_2$ on state types $\\mathrm{State}_1$ and $\\mathrm{State}_2$ with a common label type $\\mathrm{Label}$, if a relation $r : \\mathrm{State}_1 \\to \\mathrm{State}_2 \\to \\mathrm{Prop}$ is a bisimulation between $\\mathrm{lts}_1$ and $\\mathrm{lts}_2$, then $r$ is also a simulation from $\\mathrm{lts}_1$ to $\\mathrm{lts}_2$.\n   `Cslib.LTS.IsBisimulation.isSimulation.{u_1, u_2, u_3} {State₁ : Type u_1} {State₂ : Type u_2} {Label : Type u_3}   {lts₁ : Cslib.LTS State₁ Label} {lts₂ : Cslib.LTS State₂ Label} {r : State₁ → State₂ → Prop} :   lts₁.IsBisimulation lts₂ r → lts₁.IsSimulation lts₂ r`\n   at: Cslib.Foundations.Semantics.LTS.Bisimulation:100\n   file: /home/auras/Projects/Lean_Refactor/competition/projects/v4.33/.lake/packages/cslib/Cslib/Foundations/Semantics/LTS/Bisimulation.lean\n\nLower distance is closer."}],"structured_content":{"hits":[{"source":"cslib","distance":0.6665717363357544,"name":"Cslib.LTS.HomBisimilarity.symm_simulation","kind":"theorem","moduleName":"Cslib.Foundations.Semantics.LTS.Bisimulation","signature":"Cslib.LTS.HomBisimilarity.symm_simulation.{u_4, u_5} {State✝ : Type u_4} {Label✝ : Type u_5}\n  {lts : Cslib.LTS State✝ Label✝} : lts.HomBisimilarity = fun s₁ s₂ => ∃ r, r s₁ s₂ ∧ Std.Symm r ∧ lts.IsHomSimulation r","docstring":"Homogeneous bisimilarity can also be characterized through symmetric simulations. ","informalName":"Homogeneous bisimilarity as symmetric simulation","informalDescription":"For a labelled transition system $\\mathrm{lts}$ on a state type $S$ and a label type $L$, the homogeneous bisimilarity relation $\\mathrm{HomBisimilarity}$ between states $s_1$ and $s_2$ holds if and only if there exists a symmetric homogeneous simulation relation $r$ on $S$ such that $r$ relates $s_1$ and $s_2$. In other words, two states are bisimilar exactly when they are related by some symmetric simulation.","sourceStart":431,"sourceStop":443,"localPath":"/home/auras/Projects/Lean_Refactor/competition/projects/v4.33/.lake/packages/cslib/Cslib/Foundations/Semantics/LTS/Bisimulation.lean","githubUrl":"https://github.com/leanprover/cslib/blob/v4.32.0/Cslib/Foundations/Semantics/LTS/Bisimulation.lean#L431-L443"},{"source":"cslib","distance":0.6769439578056335,"name":"Cslib.LTS.IsBisimulation.isSimulation_iff","kind":"theorem","moduleName":"Cslib.Foundations.Semantics.LTS.Bisimulation","signature":"Cslib.LTS.IsBisimulation.isSimulation_iff.{u_1, u_2, u_3} {State₁ : Type u_1} {State₂ : Type u_2} {Label : Type u_3}\n  {lts₁ : Cslib.LTS State₁ Label} {lts₂ : Cslib.LTS State₂ Label} {r : State₁ → State₂ → Prop} :\n  lts₁.IsBisimulation lts₂ r ↔ lts₁.IsSimulation lts₂ r ∧ lts₂.IsSimulation lts₁ (flip r)","docstring":"A relation is a bisimulation iff both it and its inverse are simulations. ","informalName":"Bisimulation iff both it and its inverse are simulations","informalDescription":"For two labelled transition systems $\\mathrm{lts}_1$ and $\\mathrm{lts}_2$ on state types $\\mathrm{State}_1$ and $\\mathrm{State}_2$ with a common label type $\\mathrm{Label}$, and a relation $r : \\mathrm{State}_1 \\to \\mathrm{State}_2 \\to \\mathrm{Prop}$, the relation $r$ is a bisimulation between $\\mathrm{lts}_1$ and $\\mathrm{lts}_2$ if and only if $r$ is a simulation from $\\mathrm{lts}_1$ to $\\mathrm{lts}_2$ and the inverse relation $r^{-1}$ (defined by $r^{-1}(s_2, s_1) := r(s_1, s_2)$) is a simulation from $\\mathrm{lts}_2$ to $\\mathrm{lts}_1$.","sourceStart":104,"sourceStop":108,"localPath":"/home/auras/Projects/Lean_Refactor/competition/projects/v4.33/.lake/packages/cslib/Cslib/Foundations/Semantics/LTS/Bisimulation.lean","githubUrl":"https://github.com/leanprover/cslib/blob/v4.32.0/Cslib/Foundations/Semantics/LTS/Bisimulation.lean#L104-L108"},{"source":"cslib","distance":0.7055403590202332,"name":"Cslib.LTS.Bisimilarity.symm","kind":"theorem","moduleName":"Cslib.Foundations.Semantics.LTS.Bisimulation","signature":"Cslib.LTS.Bisimilarity.symm.{u_3, u_4} {Label : Type u_3} {State : Type u_4} {lts₁ lts₂ : Cslib.LTS State Label}\n  {s₁ s₂ : State} (h : lts₁.Bisimilarity lts₂ s₁ s₂) : lts₂.Bisimilarity lts₁ s₂ s₁","docstring":"Bisimilarity is symmetric. ","informalName":"Symmetry of Bisimilarity","informalDescription":"Bisimilarity is symmetric: for any labelled transition systems $\\mathrm{lts}_1$ and $\\mathrm{lts}_2$ on the same state type $S$ and label type $L$, if states $s_1$ and $s_2$ are bisimilar in $\\mathrm{lts}_1$ and $\\mathrm{lts}_2$ respectively, then $s_2$ and $s_1$ are bisimilar in $\\mathrm{lts}_2$ and $\\mathrm{lts}_1$ respectively.","sourceStart":152,"sourceStop":156,"localPath":"/home/auras/Projects/Lean_Refactor/competition/projects/v4.33/.lake/packages/cslib/Cslib/Foundations/Semantics/LTS/Bisimulation.lean","githubUrl":"https://github.com/leanprover/cslib/blob/v4.32.0/Cslib/Foundations/Semantics/LTS/Bisimulation.lean#L152-L156"},{"source":"cslib","distance":0.7266942262649536,"name":"Cslib.LTS.IsBisimulation","kind":"definition","moduleName":"Cslib.Foundations.Semantics.LTS.Bisimulation","signature":"Cslib.LTS.IsBisimulation.{u_1, u_2, u_3} {State₁ : Type u_1} {State₂ : Type u_2} {Label : Type u_3}\n  (lts₁ : Cslib.LTS State₁ Label) (lts₂ : Cslib.LTS State₂ Label) (r : State₁ → State₂ → Prop) : Prop","docstring":"A relation is a bisimulation if, whenever it relates two states,\nthe transitions originating from these states mimic each other and the reached\nderivatives are themselves related. ","informalName":"Bisimulation relation for labelled transition systems","informalDescription":"Given two labelled transition systems $\\mathrm{lts}_1$ and $\\mathrm{lts}_2$ on state types $\\mathrm{State}_1$ and $\\mathrm{State}_2$ with a common label type $\\mathrm{Label}$, a relation $r : \\mathrm{State}_1 \\to \\mathrm{State}_2 \\to \\mathrm{Prop}$ is a bisimulation if, whenever $r$ relates two states $s_1$ and $s_2$, then for every label $\\mu$, the following two conditions hold:\n\n1. For every transition $s_1 \\xrightarrow{\\mu} s_1'$ in $\\mathrm{lts}_1$, there exists a transition $s_2 \\xrightarrow{\\mu} s_2'$ in $\\mathrm{lts}_2$ such that $r$ relates $s_1'$ and $s_2'$.\n2. For every transition $s_2 \\xrightarrow{\\mu} s_2'$ in $\\mathrm{lts}_2$, there exists a transition $s_1 \\xrightarrow{\\mu} s_1'$ in $\\mathrm{lts}_1$ such that $r$ relates $s_1'$ and $s_2'$.\n\nIn other words, the transitions from related states mimic each other, and the reached derivatives are again related.","sourceStart":75,"sourceStop":84,"localPath":"/home/auras/Projects/Lean_Refactor/competition/projects/v4.33/.lake/packages/cslib/Cslib/Foundations/Semantics/LTS/Bisimulation.lean","githubUrl":"https://github.com/leanprover/cslib/blob/v4.32.0/Cslib/Foundations/Semantics/LTS/Bisimulation.lean#L75-L84"},{"source":"cslib","distance":0.7287125587463379,"name":"Cslib.LTS.IsBisimulation.simulationEquiv","kind":"theorem","moduleName":"Cslib.Foundations.Semantics.LTS.Bisimulation","signature":"Cslib.LTS.IsBisimulation.simulationEquiv.{u_1, u_2, u_3} {State₁ : Type u_1} {State₂ : Type u_2} {Label : Type u_3}\n  {lts₁ : Cslib.LTS State₁ Label} {lts₂ : Cslib.LTS State₂ Label} {r : State₁ → State₂ → Prop} {s₁ : State₁}\n  {s₂ : State₂} (h : lts₁.IsBisimulation lts₂ r) (hrel : r s₁ s₂) : lts₁.SimulationEquiv lts₂ s₁ s₂","docstring":"Bisimulation implies simulation equivalence. ","informalName":"Bisimulation implies simulation equivalence","informalDescription":"If a relation $r$ between the states of two labelled transition systems is a bisimulation, then any two states related by $r$ are simulation equivalent.","sourceStart":187,"sourceStop":189,"localPath":"/home/auras/Projects/Lean_Refactor/competition/projects/v4.33/.lake/packages/cslib/Cslib/Foundations/Semantics/LTS/Bisimulation.lean","githubUrl":"https://github.com/leanprover/cslib/blob/v4.32.0/Cslib/Foundations/Semantics/LTS/Bisimulation.lean#L187-L189"},{"source":"cslib","distance":0.7301706671714783,"name":"Cslib.LTS.IsBisimulationUpTo","kind":"definition","moduleName":"Cslib.Foundations.Semantics.LTS.Bisimulation","signature":"Cslib.LTS.IsBisimulationUpTo.{u_1, u_2, u_3} {State₁ : Type u_1} {State₂ : Type u_2} {Label : Type u_3}\n  (lts₁ : Cslib.LTS State₁ Label) (lts₂ : Cslib.LTS State₂ Label) (r : State₁ → State₂ → Prop) : Prop","docstring":"A relation `r` is a bisimulation up to homogeneous bisimilarity if, whenever it relates two\nstates in an lts, the transitions originating from these states mimic each other and the reached\nderivatives are themselves related by `r` up to bisimilarity. ","informalName":"Bisimulation up to homogeneous bisimilarity","informalDescription":"A relation $r$ between the states of two labelled transition systems $\\mathrm{lts}_1$ and $\\mathrm{lts}_2$ (with the same label type) is a bisimulation up to homogeneous bisimilarity if, whenever $r$ relates two states $s_1$ and $s_2$, then for every label $\\mu$, every transition from $s_1$ labelled by $\\mu$ to some $s_1'$ is matched by a transition from $s_2$ labelled by $\\mu$ to some $s_2'$ such that $s_1'$ and $s_2'$ are related by the relation 'up to homogeneous bisimilarity' (i.e., the composition of homogeneous bisimilarity on $\\mathrm{lts}_1$, the relation $r$, and homogeneous bisimilarity on $\\mathrm{lts}_2$), and symmetrically, every transition from $s_2$ labelled by $\\mu$ is matched by a transition from $s_1$ labelled by $\\mu$ to a state related up to homogeneous bisimilarity.","sourceStart":269,"sourceStop":280,"localPath":"/home/auras/Projects/Lean_Refactor/competition/projects/v4.33/.lake/packages/cslib/Cslib/Foundations/Semantics/LTS/Bisimulation.lean","githubUrl":"https://github.com/leanprover/cslib/blob/v4.32.0/Cslib/Foundations/Semantics/LTS/Bisimulation.lean#L269-L280"},{"source":"cslib","distance":0.7331397533416748,"name":"Cslib.LTS.IsHomBisimulation","kind":"definition","moduleName":"Cslib.Foundations.Semantics.LTS.Bisimulation","signature":"Cslib.LTS.IsHomBisimulation.{u_3, u_4} {Label : Type u_3} {State : Type u_4} (lts : Cslib.LTS State Label)\n  (r : State → State → Prop) : Prop","docstring":"A homogeneous bisimulation is a bisimulation where the underlying LTSs are the same. ","informalName":"Homogeneous bisimulation relation for a labelled transition system","informalDescription":"Given a labelled transition system $\\mathrm{lts}$ on a state type $\\mathrm{State}$ and a label type $\\mathrm{Label}$, a relation $r : \\mathrm{State} \\to \\mathrm{State} \\to \\mathrm{Prop}$ is a homogeneous bisimulation if it is a bisimulation between $\\mathrm{lts}$ and itself. That is, whenever $r$ relates two states $s_1$ and $s_2$, then for every label $\\mu$, the following two conditions hold:\n\n1. For every transition $s_1 \\xrightarrow{\\mu} s_1'$ in $\\mathrm{lts}$, there exists a transition $s_2 \\xrightarrow{\\mu} s_2'$ in $\\mathrm{lts}$ such that $r$ relates $s_1'$ and $s_2'$.\n2. For every transition $s_2 \\xrightarrow{\\mu} s_2'$ in $\\mathrm{lts}$, there exists a transition $s_1 \\xrightarrow{\\mu} s_1'$ in $\\mathrm{lts}$ such that $r$ relates $s_1'$ and $s_2'$.","sourceStart":110,"sourceStop":111,"localPath":"/home/auras/Projects/Lean_Refactor/competition/projects/v4.33/.lake/packages/cslib/Cslib/Foundations/Semantics/LTS/Bisimulation.lean","githubUrl":"https://github.com/leanprover/cslib/blob/v4.32.0/Cslib/Foundations/Semantics/LTS/Bisimulation.lean#L110-L111"},{"source":"cslib","distance":0.7415674328804016,"name":"Cslib.LTS.IsBisimulation.isSimulation","kind":"theorem","moduleName":"Cslib.Foundations.Semantics.LTS.Bisimulation","signature":"Cslib.LTS.IsBisimulation.isSimulation.{u_1, u_2, u_3} {State₁ : Type u_1} {State₂ : Type u_2} {Label : Type u_3}\n  {lts₁ : Cslib.LTS State₁ Label} {lts₂ : Cslib.LTS State₂ Label} {r : State₁ → State₂ → Prop} :\n  lts₁.IsBisimulation lts₂ r → lts₁.IsSimulation lts₂ r","docstring":"Any bisimulation is also a simulation. ","informalName":"Bisimulation implies simulation","informalDescription":"For any labelled transition systems $\\mathrm{lts}_1$ and $\\mathrm{lts}_2$ on state types $\\mathrm{State}_1$ and $\\mathrm{State}_2$ with a common label type $\\mathrm{Label}$, if a relation $r : \\mathrm{State}_1 \\to \\mathrm{State}_2 \\to \\mathrm{Prop}$ is a bisimulation between $\\mathrm{lts}_1$ and $\\mathrm{lts}_2$, then $r$ is also a simulation from $\\mathrm{lts}_1$ to $\\mathrm{lts}_2$.","sourceStart":100,"sourceStop":102,"localPath":"/home/auras/Projects/Lean_Refactor/competition/projects/v4.33/.lake/packages/cslib/Cslib/Foundations/Semantics/LTS/Bisimulation.lean","githubUrl":"https://github.com/leanprover/cslib/blob/v4.32.0/Cslib/Foundations/Semantics/LTS/Bisimulation.lean#L100-L102"}],"notice":null}},"error":null,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,45p' Arena/Cslib_CCS_bisimilarity_congr_choice.lean\nsed -n '255,310p' Arena/Cslib_CCS_bisimilarity_congr_choice.lean\nrg -n 'follow_fst|bisimulation_of|symmetric|le_bisimilarity|namespace HomBisimilarity' .lake/packages/cslib/Cslib/ -g '*.lean'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,45p' Arena/Cslib_CCS_bisimilarity_congr_choice.lean\nsed -n '255,310p' Arena/Cslib_CCS_bisimilarity_congr_choice.lean\nrg -n 'follow_fst|bisimulation_of|symmetric|le_bisimilarity|namespace HomBisimilarity' .lake/packages/cslib/Cslib/ -g '*.lean'\"","aggregated_output":"/-\nCopyright (c) 2025 Fabrizio Montesi. All rights reserved.\nReleased under Apache 2.0 license as described in the file LICENSE.\nAuthors: Fabrizio Montesi\n-/\n\nmodule\n\npublic import Cslib.Foundations.Semantics.LTS.Bisimulation\npublic import Cslib.Foundations.Syntax.Congruence\npublic import Cslib.Languages.CCS.Semantics\n\n/-! # Behavioural theory of CCS\n\n## Main results\n\n- `CCS.bisimilarityCongruence`: bisimilarity is a congruence in CCS.\n\nAdditionally, some standard laws of bisimilarity for CCS, including:\n- `CCS.bisimilarity_par_nil`: P | 𝟎 ~ P.\n- `CCS.bisimilarity_par_comm`: P | Q ~ Q | P\n- `CCS.bisimilarity_choice_comm`: P + Q ~ Q + P\n-/\n\n@[expose] public section\n\nnamespace Cslib\n\nsection CCS.BehaviouralTheory\n\nopen LTS\n\nvariable {Name : Type u} {Constant : Type v} {defs : Constant → CCS.Process Name Constant → Prop}\n\nnamespace CCS\n\nopen Process Act Act.Co Context\n\nattribute [local grind] Tr\n\n@[local grind]\nprivate inductive ParNil : Process Name Constant → Process Name Constant → Prop where\n| parNil : ParNil (par p nil) p\n\n/-- P | 𝟎 ~ P -/\n    (p ~[lts (defs := defs)] q) → (pre μ p) ~[lts (defs := defs)] (pre μ q) := by\n  intro hpq\n  exists @PreBisim _ _ defs\n  constructor\n  · grind\n  intro s1 s2 hr μ'\n  cases hr\n  case pre p' q' μ hbis =>\n    unfold lts\n    constructor <;> intro _ _ <;> [exists q'; exists p'] <;> grind\n  case bisim => grind [IsBisimulation, IsBisimulation.le_bisimilarity]\n\n@[local grind]\nprivate inductive ResBisim : Process Name Constant → Process Name Constant → Prop where\n| res : (p ~[lts (defs := defs)] q) → ResBisim (res a p) (res a q)\n-- | bisim : (p ~[lts (defs := defs)] q) → ResBisim p q\n\n/-- P ~ Q → (ν a) P ~ (ν a) Q -/\ntheorem bisimilarity_congr_res :\n    (p ~[lts (defs := defs)] q) → (res a p) ~[lts (defs := defs)] (res a q) := by\n  intro hpq\n  exists @ResBisim _ _ defs\n  constructor\n  · grind\n  intro s1 s2 hr μ'\n  cases hr\n  rename_i p q a h\n  constructor\n  case left =>\n    intro s1' htr\n    cases htr with | res _ _ htr =>\n    obtain ⟨q', _, bisim⟩ := h.follow_fst htr\n    exists res a q'\n    unfold lts at *\n    #adaptation_note\n    /-- A grind regression found moving to nightly-2026-03-31 (changes from lean#13166) -/\n    split_ands\n    · grind\n    · exact ResBisim.res bisim\n  case right =>\n    intro s2' htr\n    cases htr with | res _ _ htr =>\n    obtain ⟨p', _, bisim⟩ := h.follow_snd htr\n    exists res a p'\n    unfold lts at *\n    #adaptation_note\n    /-- A grind regression found moving to nightly-2026-03-31 (changes from lean#13166) -/\n    split_ands\n    · grind\n    · exact ResBisim.res bisim\n\nprivate inductive ChoiceBisim : Process Name Constant → Process Name Constant → Prop where\n| choice : (p ~[lts (defs := defs)] q) → ChoiceBisim (choice p r) (choice q r)\n| bisim : (p ~[lts (defs := defs)] q) → ChoiceBisim p q\n\n\n.lake/packages/cslib/Cslib/MachineLearning/PACLearning/Defs.lean:67:- `hypothesisError`: the symmetric-difference error `P(h ∆ c)`.\n.lake/packages/cslib/Cslib/MachineLearning/PACLearning/Defs.lean:474:The symmetric-difference error `P(h ∆ c)` is the natural error metric, and it decomposes\n.lake/packages/cslib/Cslib/MachineLearning/PACLearning/Defs.lean:483:/-- The *symmetric-difference error* of a hypothesis `h` with respect to a target concept `c`\n.lake/packages/cslib/Cslib/Languages/CombinatoryLogic/Confluence.lean:19:`MJoin Red` is symmetric (trivially) and reflexive (since `↠` is), so we in fact show that\n.lake/packages/cslib/Cslib/Foundations/Semantics/LTS/Bisimulation.lean:54:- `IsBisimulation.le_bisimilarity`: bisimilarity is the largest bisimulation.\n.lake/packages/cslib/Cslib/Foundations/Semantics/LTS/Bisimulation.lean:61:- `Bisimilarity.symm_simulation`: bisimilarity can be characterized through symmetric simulations.\n.lake/packages/cslib/Cslib/Foundations/Semantics/LTS/Bisimulation.lean:87:theorem IsBisimulation.follow_fst\n.lake/packages/cslib/Cslib/Foundations/Semantics/LTS/Bisimulation.lean:133:theorem Bisimilarity.follow_fst (hr : s₁ ~[lts₁,lts₂] s₂) (htr : lts₁.Tr s₁ μ s₁') :\n.lake/packages/cslib/Cslib/Foundations/Semantics/LTS/Bisimulation.lean:152:/-- Bisimilarity is symmetric. -/\n.lake/packages/cslib/Cslib/Foundations/Semantics/LTS/Bisimulation.lean:206:theorem IsBisimulation.le_bisimilarity (h : IsBisimulation lts₁ lts₂ r) :\n.lake/packages/cslib/Cslib/Foundations/Semantics/LTS/Bisimulation.lean:214:    (Bisimilarity lts₁ lts₂) ⊔ r = Bisimilarity lts₁ lts₂ := sup_eq_left.mpr h.le_bisimilarity\n.lake/packages/cslib/Cslib/Foundations/Semantics/LTS/Bisimulation.lean:257:  le_top r := r.property.le_bisimilarity\n.lake/packages/cslib/Cslib/Foundations/Semantics/LTS/Bisimulation.lean:291:    obtain ⟨s₁b', hs₁b'tr, hs₁b'r⟩ := hr1b.follow_fst htr1\n.lake/packages/cslib/Cslib/Foundations/Semantics/LTS/Bisimulation.lean:293:    obtain ⟨s₂', hs₂btr, hs₂br⟩ := hr2b.follow_fst hs₂b'tr\n.lake/packages/cslib/Cslib/Foundations/Semantics/LTS/Bisimulation.lean:431:/-- Homogeneous bisimilarity can also be characterized through symmetric simulations. -/\n.lake/packages/cslib/Cslib/Foundations/Semantics/LTS/Bisimulation.lean:555:/-- Weak bisimilarity is symmetric. -/\n.lake/packages/cslib/Cslib/Foundations/Semantics/LTS/TraceEq.lean:121:/-- Trace equivalence is symmetric. -/\n.lake/packages/cslib/Cslib/Foundations/Semantics/LTS/Simulation.lean:149:/-- Simulation equivalence is symmetric. -/\n.lake/packages/cslib/Cslib/Languages/CCS/Basic.lean:74:/-- `Act.Co` is symmetric. -/\n.lake/packages/cslib/Cslib/Languages/CCS/BehaviouralTheory.lean:265:  case bisim => grind [IsBisimulation, IsBisimulation.le_bisimilarity]\n.lake/packages/cslib/Cslib/Languages/CCS/BehaviouralTheory.lean:286:    obtain ⟨q', _, bisim⟩ := h.follow_fst htr\n.lake/packages/cslib/Cslib/Languages/CCS/BehaviouralTheory.lean:326:        obtain ⟨s2', htr2, hr2⟩ := hb.follow_fst hr htr\n.lake/packages/cslib/Cslib/Languages/CCS/BehaviouralTheory.lean:331:          apply hb.le_bisimilarity _ _ hr2\n.lake/packages/cslib/Cslib/Languages/CCS/BehaviouralTheory.lean:340:      obtain ⟨s2', htr2, hr2⟩ := hb.follow_fst hr htr\n.lake/packages/cslib/Cslib/Languages/CCS/BehaviouralTheory.lean:345:      apply hb.le_bisimilarity _ _ hr2\n.lake/packages/cslib/Cslib/Languages/CCS/BehaviouralTheory.lean:358:          apply hb.le_bisimilarity _ _ hr1\n.lake/packages/cslib/Cslib/Languages/CCS/BehaviouralTheory.lean:372:        apply hb.le_bisimilarity _ _ hr1\n.lake/packages/cslib/Cslib/Languages/CCS/BehaviouralTheory.lean:396:        obtain ⟨q', _⟩ := hb.follow_fst hr htr\n.lake/packages/cslib/Cslib/Languages/CCS/BehaviouralTheory.lean:403:        obtain ⟨q', _⟩ := hb.follow_fst hr htrp\n.lake/packages/cslib/Cslib/Foundations/Relation/Defs.lean:157:/-- `SymmOn s r` is true when a relation `r` is symmetric on its restriction to a set `s`. -/\n.lake/packages/cslib/Cslib/Foundations/Relation/Euclidean.lean:219:/-- For a symmetric relation, `LeftEuclidean` and `RightEuclidean` are equivalent. -/\n.lake/packages/cslib/Cslib/Foundations/Relation/Euclidean.lean:223:/-- For a symmetric relation, `LeftEuclidean` and transitivity are equivalent. -/\n.lake/packages/cslib/Cslib/Foundations/Relation/Euclidean.lean:227:/-- For a symmetric relation, `RightEuclidean` and transitivity are equivalent. -/\n.lake/packages/cslib/Cslib/Logics/Modal/Basic.lean:234:/-- The B axiom, valid for all symmetric models. -/\n.lake/packages/cslib/Cslib/Logics/Modal/Basic.lean:240:/-- Any model that admits the axiom B is symmetric. -/\n.lake/packages/cslib/Cslib/Logics/LinearLogic/CLL/Basic.lean:285:/-- Proof-relevant equivalence is symmetric. -/\n.lake/packages/cslib/Cslib/Logics/LinearLogic/CLL/Basic.lean:298:/-- Proof-irrelevant equivalence is symmetric. -/\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
